<h2>8. Root causes (why differences remain)</h2>
<ol>
  <li>
    <strong>Split-haul / helper attribution (resolved for Daud-style cases).</strong>
    Multi-driver loads are credited from OpenRoad <em>driver_routes</em>:
    solo = full load by delivery date; helper = route share by route date;
    owner on a multi-driver load = route share by delivery date.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Revenue allocation on split loads.</strong>
    When miles match but gross differs by round amounts ($100 / $200 / $300 / ~$500–$630),
    the client payroll and our proportional revenue share are using different rounding rules.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Week-boundary / load coverage (Sadler, Chambers, Viera).</strong>
    Large mile gaps mean the set of loads/routes inside Tue–Mon differs between systems.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Secondary lines (pay, fuel, PrePass).</strong>
    Pay uses TMS compensation rates × our loaded/empty split.
    Fuel uses (miles ÷ MPG) × PPG (Samsara / card).
    PrePass uses tolls on assigned trucks in the assignment window.
    Client green input cells can differ.
  </li>
</ol>

<h2>9. Bottom line for stakeholders</h2>
<div class="box">
  <p>
    For Week 34, <strong>mileage reconciles for 63 of 68 matched drivers (93%)</strong>.
    The former flagship discrepancy (Daud / unit 245) now matches the client sheet on
    <strong>both miles and total gross</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Remaining gaps are mostly explained by multi-driver revenue sharing rules and a small
    number of drivers with different load coverage — not by incorrect API field mapping
    for miles/revenue columns.
  </p>
  <p>
    Recommended next step (if closer payroll parity is required): align split-load revenue
    rounding with the client’s $100-floor / remainder rules, then re-check the 20 gross-only
    gaps and the 5 mileage outliers load-by-load.
  </p>
</div>

<p class="meta">
  End of report · Week 34 2026 Gross Profit reconciliation · Internal / client sendable
</p>

